{SECT 0 {EXCHG {PARA 229 "" 0 "" {TEXT 338 10 "Lecture 1 " }{TEXT 
455 22 "Learning to Use Maple " }}{PARA 217 "" 0 "" {TEXT 334 12 "Clic
k on a  " }{TEXT 335 1 "+" }{TEXT 334 41 "  to open a section. You may
 click on the" }{TEXT 335 2 " -" }{TEXT 334 72 " to close the section.
 This will be useful for clearing up some space.  " }}{PARA 217 "" 0 "
" {TEXT 334 178 "As you read this worksheet, experiment with the comma
nds.  Use the mouse to change symbols. Re-execute the commands by plac
ing the cursor on the line of interest and hitting the " }{TEXT 335 6 
"Return" }{TEXT 334 84 " key again. From time to time try your hand at
 typing in and executing new commands." }}}{SECT 1 {PARA 218 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 336 26 "Controlling the font size " }{TEXT 456 0 "" }}{PARA 220 
"" 0 "" {TEXT 334 215 "The size of the fonts in this worksheet may be \+
adjusted to  by clicking on one of the three magnifying glasses on the
 menu at the top of this worksheet. For a wider selection of font size
s use one of the following  " }{TEXT 335 60 "ctrl + 1, ctrl + 2, ctrl \+
+ 3, ctrl + 4, ctrl + 5, ctrl + 6. " }{TEXT 334 10 "[Note that" }{TEXT
 335 10 " ctrl + i " }{TEXT 334 19 "means to press the " }{TEXT 335 4 
"ctrl" }{TEXT 334 5 " and " }{TEXT 335 2 "i " }{TEXT 334 23 "keys at t
he same time.]" }}{PARA 219 "" 0 "" {TEXT 337 0 "" }}{PARA 220 "" 0 ""
 {TEXT 334 122 "You may wish to use a larger font size while working o
n the worksheet and a smaller size, for example, the smallest size (" 
}{TEXT 335 8 "ctrl + 1" }{TEXT 334 61 ") when printing to save paper a
nd make your notebook thinner." }}{PARA 219 "" 0 "" {TEXT 337 0 "" }}}
{SECT 1 {PARA 218 "" 0 "" {TEXT 336 39 "What is Maple? Where does it c
ome from?" }}{PARA 219 "" 0 "" {TEXT 337 0 "" }}{EXCHG {PARA 220 "" 0 
"" {TEXT 334 11 "Maple is a " }{TEXT 335 23 "Computer Algebra System" 
}{TEXT 334 2 " (" }{TEXT 335 3 "CAS" }{TEXT 334 7 ") from " }{HYPERLNK
 17 "Waterloo Maple Software (WMS)" 4 "http://www.maplesoft.com/flash/
index.html" "" }{TEXT 334 73 ". It was originally developed by members
 of the University of Waterloo's " }{HYPERLNK 17 "Symbolic Computation
 Group (SCG)" 4 "http://www.scg.uwaterloo.ca/" "" }{TEXT 334 127 ". On
going research and development is now a combined effort between SCG, W
MS, and several research organizations including the " }{HYPERLNK 17 "
Centre for Experimental and Constructive Mathematics (CECM)" 4 "http:/
/www.cecm.sfu.ca/" "" }{TEXT 334 44 " of Simon Fraser University, Cana
da and the " }{HYPERLNK 17 "Institute of Computational Science" 4 "htt
p://www.wr.inf.ethz.ch/" "" }{TEXT 334 29 " of ETH Z\374rich, Switzerl
and. " }}{PARA 219 "" 0 "" {TEXT 337 0 "" }}{PARA 220 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
335 5 "Maple" }{TEXT 334 25 " manipulates information " }{TEXT 335 12 
"symbolically" }{TEXT 334 4 " or " }{TEXT 335 13 "algebraically" }
{TEXT 334 95 " as opposed to just numerically.  Nevertheless, it does \+
have numerical manipulation abilities. " }{TEXT 335 9 "Maple 15 " }
{TEXT 334 280 "is the newest version of Maple with many features. It i
s the ultimate productivity tool for solving mathematical problems and
 creating interactive technical documents. All major aspects of Maple \+
have been significantly enhanced to dramatically improve the user expe
rience, making " }{TEXT 404 8 "Maple 15" }{TEXT 334 71 " the most powe
rful and easiest-to-use mathematical tool on the market. " }{TEXT 405 
9 "Maple 14 " }{TEXT 334 21 "was a version before " }{TEXT 406 8 "Mapl
e 15" }{TEXT 334 49 ". Significant changes were made when going from  
" }{TEXT 335 7 "Maple V" }{TEXT 334 4 " to " }{TEXT 335 7 "Maple 6" }
{TEXT 334 6 ", and " }{TEXT 407 9 "Maple 10 " }{TEXT 334 76 "introduce
d a new document mode as part of the standard interface written in " }
{TEXT 408 4 "Java" }{TEXT 334 21 " and introduced with " }{TEXT 409 7 
"Maple 9" }{TEXT 334 124 ", so you should be sure of the version you a
re using. Some differences in syntax will be pointed out as we proceed
. See the " }{HYPERLNK 17 "Maple Links" 4 "http://www.math.usf.edu/~ma
wx/maple_links.html" "" }{TEXT 334 107 " on the course webpage for mor
e information about the history of Maple and Maple resources on the in
ternet." }}{PARA 219 "" 0 "" {TEXT 337 0 "" }}{PARA 220 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
334 68 "For those who are interested, the kernel of Maple is written i
n the " }{TEXT 335 2 "C " }{TEXT 334 150 "language. But most of the fu
nctions in Maple are written in Maple and can be viewed and manipulate
d by the user. We will discuss how to do this later." }}{PARA 219 "" 0
 "" {TEXT 337 0 "" }}{PARA 220 "" 0 "" {TEXT 334 36 "Perhaps the progr
am most similar to " }{TEXT 335 5 "Maple" }{TEXT 334 4 " is " }{TEXT 
335 11 "Mathematica" }{TEXT 334 183 ". However, there are many other s
uch programs available. Some like Maple, Mathematica and MuPAD are qui
te general and some are more specialized. For a more comprehensive lis
t see the " }{HYPERLNK 17 "List of Computer Algebra Systems" 4 "http:/
/sal.kachinatech.com/A/1/ " "" }{TEXT 334 21 " by Herng-Jeng Jou.  " }
}}{EXCHG {PARA 203 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 249 0 "" }}}}{SECT 1 {PARA 218
 "" 0 "" {TEXT 336 21 "Maple as a calculator" }}{PARA 220 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 334 30 "Maple may be used as simply a " }{TEXT 335 10 "calculato
r" }{TEXT 334 108 " with graphing capabilities:  We illustrate this ca
pability first. Later we will show how to use Maple as a " }{TEXT 335 
21 "programming language." }{TEXT 334 1 "\n" }}{PARA 220 "" 0 "" {TEXT
 334 100 "To start Maple on most platforms just double click on the Ma
ple icon. (In Unix systems one may type " }{TEXT 335 6 "xmaple" }{TEXT
 334 83 " from a command line to open Maple). When Maple opens, you ar
e in what is called a " }{TEXT 335 15 "Maple worksheet" }{TEXT 334 45 
".  Names of Maple worksheets usually end in  " }{TEXT 335 4 ".mws" }
{TEXT 334 5 " and " }{TEXT 335 5 ".mw, " }{TEXT 334 38 "which can work
 for all versions after " }{TEXT 335 7 "Maple 9" }{TEXT 410 2 ".5" }
{TEXT 334 2 ". " }}{PARA 219 "" 0 "" {TEXT 337 0 "" }}{PARA 202 "" 0 "
" {TEXT 250 355 "\"Execute\" each of the commands (in red) below by pl
acing the cursor anywhere on the line, then hitting the return key.  I
f you have done this correctly you should see the answer in blue. Also
 the cursor will move to the next line. Hit the return key again to ex
ecute the next line. Continue to do this. The first commands show how \+
to do simple arithmetic." }}{PARA 219 "" 0 "" {TEXT 337 0 "" }}{PARA 
201 "" 0 "" {TEXT 244 48 "Note that each command ends with a semi-colo
n.  " }}{PARA 219 "" 0 "" {TEXT 337 0 "" }}{PARA 220 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
334 82 "Try to guess the output, before executing a command when possi
ble. Note that this " }{TEXT 245 3 "tex" }{TEXT 334 5 "t is " }{TEXT 
335 5 "black" }{TEXT 334 6 ", the " }{TEXT 245 5 "input" }{TEXT 334 4 
" is " }{TEXT 335 3 "red" }{TEXT 334 9 " and the " }{TEXT 245 6 "outpu
t" }{TEXT 334 48 " is blue. One can enter the text mode by either " }
{TEXT 335 6 "ctrl-T" }{TEXT 334 18 ", clicking on the " }{TEXT 335 1 "
T" }{TEXT 334 35 " in the menu above, or by going to " }{TEXT 335 6 "I
nsert" }{TEXT 334 27 " on the menu and selecting " }{TEXT 335 4 "Text"
 }{TEXT 334 14 ".  Look under " }{TEXT 335 6 "Insert" }{TEXT 334 62 " \+
again to see the other options. See the section below titled " }{TEXT 
335 36 "Inserting prompts and shortcut keys." }{TEXT 334 0 "" }}{PARA 
219 "" 0 "" {TEXT 337 0 "" }}{EXCHG {PARA 200 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
246 4 "3*4;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"#7" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 200
 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 246 4 "2^5;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"#
K" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 200 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 246 8 "2^(1/3);" }}{PARA 
11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "*$)\"\"##\"\"\"\"\"$F&" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 200 ">
 " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 246 5 "12/2;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"\"'
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 200 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 246 5 "11/2;" }}{PARA 11 ""
 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "#\"#6\"\"#" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 200 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 246 7 "11.0/2;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "$\"+++++b!\"*" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 203 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 249 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 202 "" 
0 "" {TEXT 250 179 "Notice the effect of changing 11 to 11.0. Generall
y Maple uses exact arithmetic unless you use numbers containing decima
l points and then it switches to floating point arithmetic." }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 200 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 246 4 "3-4;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "!\"\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 200 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 246 4 "3+
4;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"\"(" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 202 "" 0 ""
 {TEXT 250 360 "To enhance readability, you may stretch a command acro
ss several lines and you may insert spaces. But be careful, this is no
t allowed everywhere. For example, you cannot type the number 1000000 \+
as 1 000 000; but you may group the digits into groups of three by typ
ing 1                                                                 \+
                          " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 200 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
246 2 "1\n" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "\"\"\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
220 "" 0 "" {TEXT 335 11 "IMPORTANT: " }{TEXT 247 24 "Maple is case se
nsitive." }{TEXT 334 102 " So, for example, ABC, ABc, AbC, Abc, aBc, A
bc, etc, are all different so far as Maple is concerned. \n" }{TEXT 
334 1 "\n" }{TEXT 334 11 "Maple uses " }{TEXT 335 2 "Pi" }{TEXT 334 
31 " (not pi) to denote the number " }{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting:-mrow
(Typesetting:-mi(\"pi\", italic = \"false\", mathvariant = \"normal\")
);" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6#-I#miGF$6%
Q#piF'/%'italicGQ&falseF'/%,mathvariantGQ'normalF'" }{TEXT 334 44 ". W
e can get a floating point evaluation of " }{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting
:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"pi\", italic = \"false\", mathvariant = \"nor
mal\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6#-I#m
iGF$6%Q#piF'/%'italicGQ&falseF'/%,mathvariantGQ'normalF'" }{TEXT 334 
16 " by the command " }{TEXT 335 10 "evalf(Pi) " }{TEXT 334 29 "as in \+
the following examples:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 200 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 246 
3 "Pi;" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" {XPPMATH 20 "I#PiG%*protectedG" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 200 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 246 10 "evalf(Pi);" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "$\"+aEfTJ!\"*" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 200 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
246 33 "evalf(pi); #pi is not equal to Pi" }}{PARA 11 "" 1 "" 
{XPPMATH 20 "I#piG6\"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 203 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 249 0 
"" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 220 "" 0 "" {TEXT 334 6 "Note  " }{TEXT 335 1 "#" }
{TEXT 334 63 " may be used for comments as we just did. Everything fol
lowing " }{TEXT 335 1 "#" }{TEXT 334 89 " on a line is ignored by Mapl
e. However, it looks better to use the text mode. Note that " }{TEXT 
